# Every tracked text file is stored with LF and checked out with LF, on every
# platform and whatever a clone's own core.autocrlf says.
#
# Without this line the line ending of a working copy is a property of each
# contributor's git configuration rather than of this repository. Measured on a
# Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true, before this file existed: a fresh
# checkout of the branch that added the build entry point produced CRLF in
# every Go file, and
#
#     gofmt -l .
#
# listed all nine of them. The formatting leg of the gate would then be red on a
# clean clone for a reason that is in nobody's diff, which is the specific
# failure the build entry point is supposed to make impossible.
#
# The corpus is the other half. A record is a transcription of a published
# number, and byte-for-byte reproducibility of anything built from it cannot be
# a property of the operating system somebody cloned on. The hazard was named on
# the reproducibility issue as untested; this removes it rather than tests it.
* text=auto eol=lf
